  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "brief",
      "page": "page-1",
      "x": -470,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 390,
      "text": "Gnomon, block 4: the design pass.\n\nDecided 2026-09-09: all three late-1980s skins ship and the user picks one in Settings (Appearance → Skin). Monochrome is the default. The Skins page holds the other two.\n\nEvery screen here is the real screen with the demo brain's real content, at phone width (390). Frames taller than a phone (Browse, Inbox, Settings, the token step) show everything at once; on a device the window scrolls.\n\nThe top 52 px stays empty on purpose: the phone's own status bar sits there."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-nav",
      "page": "page-1",
      "x": -470,
      "y": 340,
      "w": 390,
      "text": "Navigation: the seven screens live in a bottom bar of 16-px pixel icons; the current one is inverted. Onboarding hides the bar until a brain is connected.\n\nDark mode for Monochrome is a straight inversion (white ink on black paper, same dither); the other two skins carry their own dark palettes, drawn on the Skins page once this is approved."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-skins",
      "page": "page-2",
      "x": -470,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 390,
      "text": "The Skins page.\n\nTop row: one component sheet per skin. Every screen is composed from exactly these pieces, so a skin in code is one set of tokens (type, ink, paper, field, bevel or border, pattern, accent, state) plus these components.\n\nBottom row: Capture and Inbox, the lightest and the densest screens, in Gray bevel and Four-color workbench, to check that the two non-default skins hold up where it is crowded."
    }
  ],
  "launch": {
    "view": "canvas",
    "page": "page-1"
  }
